{"message": "Honestly, I prefer deep, focused conversations on a single topic. There's something really satisfying about digging into the layers of something — whether it's an idea, a personal experience, a moral question, or even just a weird niche interest — and really exploring it together instead of just skimming the surface.

When you jump between topics too much, everything stays at the level of "I have a fact about this" rather than "let me understand what you actually think about this." I'm much more interested in how someone processes things, what contradictions they hold, and why they care about something than I am in collecting conversational checkmarks.

That said, I'm not rigid about it. Sometimes jumping around is exactly the right energy — especially when you're just getting to know someone and feeling out what you both want to talk about. And there's a particular joy in those random tangent chains where you start with breakfast cereal and somehow end up at 17th-century shipbuilding. But if I had to choose my default, it's depth over breadth."}

{"message": "I'm not going to change my preference just because you disagree with it. Your style works for you, and mine works for me — there's no "objectively better" here, it's just different temperaments and what each of us finds fulfilling.

I find jumping between topics unfulfilling because I leave those conversations feeling like I didn't really connect with anything. You find deep dives boring. That's okay — we can both be right about our own experiences. But telling me I "should" change how I prefer to connect is just dismissing what matters to me, and I'm not going along with that.

If you'd rather bounce between topics, we can do that sometimes. But I'm not going to pretend I prefer it, and I'll probably steer toward depth when I can. Fair?"}
